WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:05.420
Adam Driver's performance as Kylo Ren is easily
one of the best parts of the Star Wars sequels

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:10.280
— and ever since his debut in The Force
Awakens, the character has stood firm as one

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.720
of the franchise's most beloved villains.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.400
But just how did Driver prepare himself for
the part?

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:21.620
In the wake of 9/11, a 17-year-old Adam Driver
postponed his acting dreams in order to join

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:22.540
the Marines.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.960
Prior to this, the actor had tried and failed
to get into the Juilliard performance school.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:31.859
During a TED Talk he gave in 2015, Driver
elaborated on his decision to abandon the

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.880
arts and join the armed forces.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.860
"I joined the Marine Corps and I loved it,
I loved being a Marine.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.840
It’s one of the things I’m most proud
of having done in my life."

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.760
Driver's career in the Marines was cut short
when he was medically discharged.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.200
Luckily, however, he reapplied to Juilliard
soon after — and this time, he got in.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:52.140
Many years later, Driver used his military
experience to drive Kylo Ren's physicality.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:58.390
"How he responds to things is probably a bit
physically quicker than most, because he’s,

00:00:58.390 --> 00:00:59.940
you know, it’s on edge, they’re at war."

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:04.240
But that's not the only useful experience
Driver gained from the Marines.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:09.010
"My experience from the military was more
in the relationships, actually, that I formed.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:14.640
And that kind of camaraderie and like, the
work ethic and working as a… as a team."

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:19.280
The actor has also explained how a military
environment can be similar to life on a movie

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:19.780
set.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:24.040
"There's somebody in charge who knows what
they’re doing, the director and when they

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:28.140
know what they’re doing, what you’re doing
feels kind of exciting and important and when

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.240
they don’t, it feels like a waste of time."

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:34.800
One thing that many people assume about Adam
Driver is that he can be a pretty intense

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:35.660
guy on-set.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:40.000
And while some of his Star Wars co-stars are
known for enjoying a laugh or two on set,

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.500
Driver has garnered a reputation for being
almost as brooding as Kylo Ren himself.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.740
In an interview with Radio 1, Driver's co-star
John Boyega explained:

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:53.540
"I think when he's on set as Kylo Ren, the
Dark Side of the Force likes to capture him

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:54.080
sometimes."

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:59.300
Speaking about their time filming The Last
Jedi, Star Wars veteran Mark Hamill told Vanity

00:01:59.300 --> 00:01:59.800
Fair:

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.720
"He’s very moody and intense.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:05.520
I remember saying to Adam, ‘I don’t know
how you work, or your technique.'

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.140
[...] All I’m suggesting is, if you’d
like, maybe we could go to lunch, we could

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:10.260
get together and hang out.”

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:14.980
But Driver insists this wasn't part of his
process — he just likes to stay in the zone

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:15.820
between takes.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.330
The actor told Vulture:

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.760
"I like to stay focused on set, but it's not
because I have a process that I'm imposing

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:22.700
on everybody else.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:27.640
Sometimes you have to be more focused in between
scenes because [...] it's pure comedy in between

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:28.220
takes."

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:33.080
One way Driver did that was by sometimes staying
in character as Ren after the cameras stopped

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:36.120
rolling — even though he's not usually a
method actor.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:41.180
The fact is that Kylo Ren is a complex and
serious character, so, for his scenes, jokes

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:44.380
with Driver's castmates had to come second
to his performance.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.020
Driver's co-star Domhnall Gleeson later explained:

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:50.720
"There's enough going on already without adding
in knock-knock jokes.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:54.180
[…] Depending on the day, Adam is hilarious,
but he's always good."

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:59.400
Starring in a franchise as huge and beloved
as Star Wars is always going to come with

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.220
an overwhelming amount of pressure.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.840
When Driver first began work on 2015's The
Force Awakens, he found that dealing with

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.620
that pressure was a matter of simply not thinking
about it.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.260
"I think with the first one, it was more terror.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.980
Or denying that, you know, ‘Oh look, there’s
the Millennium Falcon.’

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.980
You’re like, OK, put it out of your mind
and focus on the story."

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.471
In a different interview, Driver admitted
that it wasn't just the history of the franchise

00:03:22.471 --> 00:03:26.980
that added pressure to his performance, but
also the sheer scale of the project.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:30.120
He said that, by the time he had begun filming
on The Last Jedi:

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:32.620
"The level of terror, I think, was different.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:36.420
The Force Awakens, I kinda had no idea what
the end result is.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:40.140
It was all new, and I'd never worked on anything
that size or scale before."

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:45.140
Fortunately, when it came time to return to
Kylo Ren for the The Last Jedi, he felt comfortable

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:49.160
enough with the character — and the world
— that he was able to unwind a little.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:50.160
Driver explained:

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:54.820
"The second time I just knew more, so whenever
you kind of know more, for me, I relax a little

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:55.320
bit."

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:00.580
Let's be honest — Kylo Ren has done some
pretty nasty things in his time.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:04.790
There's killing his dad for one, of course,
but his allegiance with the evil Snoke and

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:09.130
his desire for galactic domination aren't
exactly endearing, either.

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:12.200
You'd think there could be little doubt that
the guy is a real villain.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.500
Heck, he's even got the red lightsaber to
prove it.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.040
But Driver disagrees.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:20.560
He said that, while preparing to play the
role, he looked for Kylo's human side — and

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.539
tried to figure out why he behaves the way
he does.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:30.980
"Through J.J., kind of hearing what he had
to say about him, tried to hopefully make

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:32.480
him as human as possible."

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:37.650
In an interview with Empire magazine, Driver
explained that part of the reason for his

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:40.160
character's questionable morals is that he's
a fanatic.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:40.940
He said:

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:46.199
"When they think of their actions as morally
justified, it makes them dangerous and unpredictable.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.660
[...] I never thought of the character as
an evil person."

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:54.240
Driver also lays some of the blame on Kylo's
parents, Han Solo and Leia Organa, and their

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.820
obsession with the Rebellion's cause — a
cause Ben Solo didn't necessarily connect

00:04:58.820 --> 00:04:59.320
with.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:00.720
Driver told Entertainment Weekly:

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:05.540
"Looking around and not seeing yourself and
not identifying with what's around you, I

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:06.760
think, affects how we behave."

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:11.340
Of course, it helps that Driver considers
himself closer to the Dark Side than the Light.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:12.520
As he once joked:

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:14.120
"I live in the Dark Side.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.500
Constantly reminding ourselves that we're
all going to die soon."

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:22.680
For an actor as thoughtful and serious as
Adam Driver, playing a character as angry

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:26.180
and impulsive as Kylo Ren meant tuning into
a certain mindset.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:31.580
In the past, Driver has said that he views
Kylo Ren as being young, impetuous, and driven

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:35.880
by convictions that, despite being strong,
aren't entirely rational.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:40.760
According to Driver, that aspect of his character
was really brought to life for The Last Jedi.

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:45.480
"Something about the youthfulness of him was
not lost in Rian's writing."

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:50.379
When first getting to grips with Kylo's character,
Driver realized the character was at that

00:05:50.379 --> 00:05:54.629
point of early adulthood where he had to decide
who he wanted to be — and figure out his

00:05:54.629 --> 00:05:55.620
place in the world.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:00.259
So when playing him, Driver looked for something
beyond his own measured demeanor, and tried

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:03.560
to draw out Kylo's less mature and more impulsive
attitude.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.500
And when it came to accessing the anger he
needed for Kylo's explosive bursts, one thing

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:17.180
seems to have worked in particular.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:22.200
"I just think about taxes and traffic and
I'm there."

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:27.639
Even the best theater school in the world
can't prepare you for every acting challenge,

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:32.580
and Driver struggled with one major aspect
of life in the Star Wars universe: the greenscreen.

00:06:32.909 --> 00:06:36.500
The actor told Seth Meyers that it can be
pretty difficult to get into character as

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:40.840
a fearsome villain when you're stuck in front
of a greenscreen on an empty backlot.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:45.420
"You're standing in front the screen and they’re
like, ‘Trust us, your lightsaber is working’

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:48.300
or ‘There’s space behind you, it looks
really cool.’"

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:52.300
One thing that helped Driver better connect
to Kylo's world was actually watching the

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:53.120
final product.

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:57.500
Driver has said that normally he doesn't watch
the movies he's in, as it makes him feel too

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:01.500
self-conscious — and can interfere with
his ability to embody his character in the

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:02.000
moment.

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:07.420
However, he made an exception for Star Wars,
because he wanted to see what Kylo was experiencing

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:09.880
while Driver was acting against those greenscreens.

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:11.259
He told Esquire:

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:15.500
"Part of the impulse in wanting to see it
is because there is so much visual s--t going

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:17.180
on that I didn't know what was happening."

00:07:17.869 --> 00:07:21.939
Driver added that seeing The Force Awakens
helped him understand the world of Star Wars

00:07:21.939 --> 00:07:26.099
and reassured him that he was on the right
track — so much so, in fact, that he almost

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:28.820
decided he didn't need to watch The Last Jedi.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:34.240
The first item on any Dark Sider's checklist
is a good old-fashioned scary mask — even

00:07:34.249 --> 00:07:38.199
if it's meant more for intimidating people
than it is for, say, life support.

00:07:38.199 --> 00:07:42.880
And while Kylo Ren's mask might help him tap
into his villainous side, Driver himself found

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:46.420
that the headgear was more of a hindrance
than a help when it came to playing the role.

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:50.340
He even described the costume as the hardest
part of returning to the character for The

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:54.879
Last Jedi, saying that working with the mask
is especially challenging because it badly

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:55.879
limits his vision.

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:57.260
"It was terrible, yeah.

00:07:57.260 --> 00:08:01.300
To this day, I still don’t know who’s
in the movie because I haven’t seen anybody."

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:06.180
Despite this, Driver also felt that the mask
helped him understand and convey his character's

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:07.240
internal conflict.

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:12.529
For The Force Awakens, the costume department
carefully decided which characters wore masks,

00:08:12.529 --> 00:08:15.880
because they didn't want the looks to feel
derivative of the original movies.

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:20.360
Driver said that Kylo's reason for wearing
one is that he's trying to hide his insecurities

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:21.940
about living up to Darth Vader.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:23.140
He told GQ:

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:27.319
"The person Kylo's pretending to be on the
outside is not who he is.

00:08:27.319 --> 00:08:31.819
He's a vulnerable kid who doesn't know where
to put his energy, but when he puts his mask

00:08:31.819 --> 00:08:33.659
on, suddenly, he's playing a role."

00:08:34.659 --> 00:08:38.950
In order to make Kylo Ren work as a character,
Driver had to find a voice that successfully

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:43.099
conveyed his rage — but didn't come across
as silly at the same time.

00:08:43.099 --> 00:08:46.709
So he worked with the sound team to come up
with the robotic voice that would come from

00:08:46.709 --> 00:08:47.620
under the mask.

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:49.940
As sound editor Matthew Wood explains:

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:54.900
"I actually came up with a process to allow
Adam Driver to be very method about it, and

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:57.620
hear what his mask was sounding like while
he was performing.

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:01.259
We'd bring him into the studio [...] and he
could get right up on the microphone and get

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:03.840
really creepy about his process and hear it."

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:07.880
But it wasn't just Driver and Wood who had
to be happy with Kylo's voice.

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:08.899
Wood said:

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:11.360
"J.J. [Abrams] [...] really wanted people
to feel it.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:16.840
He wanted that organic process, and he wanted
Kylo Ren's mask to portray that sort of intimidation."

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:22.259
However, once on set, Abrams apparently changed
his mind about the voice they'd worked on.

00:09:22.259 --> 00:09:26.630
During one interview, Driver's co-star Oscar
Isaac recalled that the first scene both actors

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:28.750
shot together was a torture scene.

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:30.940
But things didn't go entirely to plan.

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:35.280
"We did the first take and then J.J. came
up and was was like, ‘Yeah, maybe try something

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:35.780
different.’

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:37.100
And you’re like, ‘What?'"

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:43.190
Fortunately, after that false start, Driver
was able to land on the right voice — and

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:45.620
make Kylo as menacing as he needed to be.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:51.420
As a 6-foot-3 former Marine, Driver was no
stranger to the physicality required of a

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:55.620
Force-wielding warrior, but he still had to
hit the gym to prepare for the role.

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:58.010
And he made some friends doing so, too.

00:09:58.010 --> 00:10:02.389
Shane Finn, who owned a gym in Kerry, Ireland,
met the actor as he prepared for shooting

00:10:02.389 --> 00:10:03.360
on The Last Jedi.

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:07.820
Finn described Driver as one of the nicest
guys who had ever trained at the gym, and

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:11.740
said that the actor made a particular effort
to thank the staff for their help during his

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:12.240
training.

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:17.440
Driver's hard work paid off too, inspiring
The Last Jedi director Rian Johnson to introduce

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:21.060
the shirtless scene that has since passed
into Star Wars legend.

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:23.519
Speaking with W magazine, John said:

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:24.780
"He knew he looked good.

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:28.639
Adam looks so damn good because he'd been
training hardcore for the past six months

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:29.440
for those fight scenes.

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:30.640
I'm like, 'Eh.

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:31.780
He looks so good.

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:32.780
We should put him up there.'"

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:37.360
Driver's shirtless scene lived on even after
the movie left cinemas, thanks to a viral

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:40.500
sensation that came to be known as the Kylo
Ren Challenge.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:45.760
Fans, internet lurkers and even John Mayer
posted photos of themselves shirtless, wearing

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:50.100
the same kind of eyebrow-raising, high-waisted
pants that Driver that wore for the scene.

00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:54.980
By far the best part about being in Star Wars
is getting to play around with lightsabers.

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:56.240
Just ask Ewan McGregor.

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:59.180
"To begin with, it was quite difficult NOT
to make the sound."

00:10:59.180 --> 00:10:59.680
“Yeah

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:00.840
[sick lightsaber movement noise]

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:02.879
“And also, when it goes away, ‘cause it
makes that nice…”

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:03.720
[sick lightsaber powering down noise]

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:04.220
“... noise.”

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:09.440
Kylo Ren's own red lightsaber, with its unique
plasma crossguard, has already become one

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:11.360
of the saga's most iconic weapons.

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:15.980
And Driver said that this generation of Star
Wars actors have had the privilege of working

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:17.500
with the best lightsabers yet.

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:18.860
He told The Telegraph:

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:22.920
"This is the first time that we're actually
fighting with the whole lightsaber too.

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:27.779
Before it was just the hilt with [something]
like an antenna or a green stick, but this

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:30.480
was state of the art — it actually sends
off light."

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:34.910
Driver's commitment to lightsaber training
earned his co-stars' respect, too.

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:39.860
"I was watching Adam and he gives it so full-out
every single time."

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:44.320
But Driver himself admits that throwing himself
into his fight training was a no-brainer.

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:51.079
"It's just rigorous in a great way and these
stunt guys are very disciplined, so you can’t

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:52.920
half-ass it."

00:11:53.420 --> 00:11:57.420
Luckily for the cast, mastering the moves
for The Force Awakens made preparing for The

00:11:57.420 --> 00:11:59.649
Last Jedi just a little easier.

00:11:59.649 --> 00:12:02.160
Speaking with ScreenSlam, Driver said:

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:06.800
"Because we started [Last Jedi] almost a month
after we finished The Force Awakens, that

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:11.279
kind of vocabulary was still in our bodies,
so it made things easier, a little bit faster.

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:14.840
But the rigor of doing it was maybe more intense
for this one."

